// DeadFunctionElimination — the reachability prune that decides WHICH functions reach the back end.
//
// A function nothing can reach is still register-allocated, framed, encoded and linked: the whole
// expensive half of the compiler runs on code that can never execute. This pass runs ONCE, at the
// target-neutral backend entry (`buildBackend`, immediately after the sugar gate), so x64, arm64 and
// wasm all inherit it rather than each having to remember to re-add it — the same argument that put
// `assertNoSugarOps` there.
//
// ⭐ **WHY IT IS A PREREQUISITE, not a nicety.** shv2 compiles `stdlib/*.maxon` source into every
// program. Without this pass, loading one stdlib module drags EVERY function in it into EVERY
// program — which adds them to all 221+
// committed goldens, costs every compile, and BREAKS the wasm and arm64 suites outright, because the
// GT/clock/IOCP substrate those modules reach is x64-windows-only (a `sleep(1)` program dies on wasm at
// `StdToWasm: emitFuncAddr: no wasm function index for '__gt_trampoline'`). Reachability fixes all
// three at once.
//
// ⭐ **WHO DECIDES WHAT, AND WHY THE TWO DECIDERS CANNOT DISAGREE.** `RuntimeUsage`
// (`StringRuntime.scanRuntimeUsage` + `noteDestructorUsage`/`noteClonerUsage`) and this pass answer two
// DIFFERENT questions, in that order:
//
//   * `RuntimeUsage` owns INSTALLATION — *does this program carry the allocator / the GT scheduler /
//     the IOCP substrate / this `__destruct_<T>` at all?* It must run BEFORE lowering, because its
//     `usesHeap`/`usesGt`/`usesIocp`/`usesStreaming` bits also gate the `.data` slots the backend
//     resolves a `globalAddr` against, and a slot cannot be laid out after the fact.
//   * This pass owns REACHABILITY — *of what was installed, what can actually be reached from the
//     program's roots?* It runs LAST, on the finished Std module, and it never installs anything.
//
// So the composition is one-way and total: usage decides the FLOOR, DFE prunes within it, and DFE can
// only ever REMOVE. There is no question both answer, hence nothing for them to disagree about. That
// asymmetry is what makes an all-or-nothing install (`installArrayRuntime` installs the whole array
// runtime as one closure, because the helpers call each other) both correct and cheap: the closure is
// the safe over-approximation, and this pass takes the slack back.
//
// ⭐ **CONSERVATIVE BEATS CLEVER.** A missed prune costs bytes; a wrong prune is a link-time panic or,
// worse, a silently missing side effect. Every reference this pass cannot SEE is therefore a ROOT, and
// there are exactly three kinds of them — see `seedRoots`. A wrong prune does at least fail LOUD on
// every backend (`resolveCallFixups: call to unknown function`, `emitCall: no wasm function index`,
// `bakeFuncAbs64Relocs: … is in no debug symbol`), never silently.
//
// ⭐ **WHAT IT DOES NOT DO: it prunes FUNCTIONS, never `.rdata`.** v1's twin (`StdDeadFunctionElimination`)
// also ran `pruneDeadGlobalData`, dropping witness/layout blobs whose label no live function loaded, and
// paid for it with a `witnessConditionalRoots` sidetable, an `initToStoredVars` write-set map, a
// second "live vars excluding inits" pre-walk, and a hand-maintained `shouldKeepGlobal` allow-list of
// name prefixes. shv2 keeps the data and prunes only code, which is what lets the root set below stay
// three lines long: because every `.rdata` function slot survives, every function a slot NAMES must
// survive too, and "is this label reachable?" never has to be asked. Dead-`.rdata` elimination is a
// separate rung with its own goldens (`.rdata` offsets move, so `RequiredData` moves with them).
//
// ⚠ **`.data` IS ALREADY DONE, AND DELIBERATELY NOT HERE** (P1.7 slice 3): a dead top-level global's slot
// is never BUILT, by `IR/Maxon/DeadGlobalElimination` filtering the arena in `compileToCodeResult`. That is
// upstream of this pass for the reason stated above — a `.data` slot cannot be laid out after the fact — so
// the two never both answer for a global, and nothing here needs a `shouldKeepGlobal` list either.
//
// Orphaned blocks and ops are LEFT BEHIND, exactly as every block-rebuilding pass leaves them
// (`IrModule.liveOpIndices`' header). `compactOps`/`compactBlocks` would reclaim them, but they cost
// an O(ops) walk plus two ops-sized arrays to release memory the module drops a few milliseconds
// later, at the end of the compile that allocated it. Nothing downstream walks the flat arrays.

// `module.functions` index → was it reached? DENSE over `0 .. funcCount-1`, which is the whole
// divergence from v1's walk: a name is hashed EXACTLY ONCE per call-graph edge — at the lookup that
// resolves it to an index — and the worklist then carries scalars. v1 pushed name STRINGS and re-hashed
// one on every pop plus a `seen` insert per edge (`StdDeadFunctionElimination.maxon:88-105`), so its
// walk paid three byte-sequence hashes per edge where this pays one.
typealias FuncIndexByName = Map with (ByteArray, FuncIndex)

// Drop every function unreachable from the program's roots, in place. Rebuilds `module.functions`;
// the dropped functions' blocks and ops stay in the flat arrays as orphans (see the header).
export function eliminateDeadFunctions(module StdModule, globalData GlobalDataTable, project Project) throws CompileError
	let byName = try indexFunctionsByName(module, project: project)

	var reached = BoolArray.create()
	reached.resize(module.funcCount())
	var worklist = FuncIndexArray.create()

	seedRoots(globalData, byName: byName, reached: reached, worklist: worklist)

	while worklist.count() > 0 'walk'
		let idx = try worklist.pop() otherwise panic("eliminateDeadFunctions: pop from a non-empty worklist")
		markCalleesOf(module, func: module.getFunc(idx), byName: byName, reached: reached, worklist: worklist)
	end 'walk'

	sweepUnreachedFunctions(module, reached: reached, project: project)
end 'eliminateDeadFunctions'

// Name → `module.functions` index. Built once: without it, resolving a callee would be a linear scan of
// every function and the whole walk would be O(functions × edges).
//
// ⚠ **A DUPLICATE NAME IS DETECTED HERE, AND `upsert` IS SILENT BY CONSTRUCTION** — a second entry takes
// the first one's index out of this map, the walk marks ONE of the two live, and the other is swept: a
// call fixup with no target, or a `__maxon_global_cleanup` that never runs, from a compile nothing
// complained about. So the collision is caught rather than merely written down, and `refuseDuplicate-
// FunctionName` decides which of its three causes it is.
//
// P1.7 slice 2a is what made the premise worth enforcing: `ModuleInit` installs from a NEW site, and it is
// the only installer that appends into the MEMOIZED `queryAllModule` module rather than into the
// post-pipeline `stdModule`, so "installed at exactly one site" acquired a second dependency — that
// `compileToCodeResult` runs at most once per `Project`, which is a property of the DRIVER and not of this
// pass (see that file's mutate-the-memo header).
//
// The check costs one integer compare per function and no hash probe: the loop inserts index `i` on
// iteration `i`, so the map's height IS `i + 1` after every insert unless one collapsed two names into
// one entry. A `contains` probe ahead of each insert would answer the same question AND hand back the
// incumbent's index for free — but it doubles the hash probes on a per-function loop to save a back-scan
// that runs at most once, on a path that then refuses the compile.
function indexFunctionsByName(module StdModule, project Project) returns FuncIndexByName throws CompileError
	var byName = FuncIndexByName.create()
	for i in 0 upto module.funcCount() 'eachFunc'
		byName.upsert(module.getFunc(i).name, value: i)
		if byName.count() != i + 1 'duplicateName'
			try refuseDuplicateFunctionName(module, project: project, laterIndex: i)
		end 'duplicateName'
	end 'eachFunc'
	return byName
end 'indexFunctionsByName'

// ⭐⭐ **THREE CAUSES SHARE ONE SYMPTOM, AND PROVENANCE IS WHAT TELLS THEM APART (A1t).** Two of them are
// compiler bugs and must stay a panic; the third is an AUTHOR's mistake and gets a diagnostic:
//
//   * **one side parsed, one side synthesized** — a declaration collides with a symbol the compiler
//     emits. E4015, positioned at the declaration. D6 is what opened the door — it let
//     `stdlib/Builtins.maxon` DECLARE reserved `__` names — and what creates the PAIR is whatever makes
//     the compiler install its own copy, which differs by runtime. ⚠ **NOT always a call:** a
//     usage-gated symbol needs one (A1r opened that door, and `__print_string` is installed by
//     `print`/an explicit call), but `__module_init`/`__maxon_global_cleanup` are installed on
//     `globals.count() != 0` alone (`ModuleInit`), so declaring one of THOSE collides with no call
//     anywhere in the program. MEASURED both ways. The check runs before the reachability walk, so an
//     uncalled declaration is diagnosed rather than quietly pruned.
//   * **both synthesized** — an installer ran twice, or one `Project` was compiled twice over the
//     memoized module. A compiler bug.
//   * **both parsed** — two declarations of one name, which `commitFuncSignatures` reports as E3006 at
//     merge and the pipeline's error gate stops long before here. A compiler bug in that check.
//
// **The discriminator is `sourceFilePath`, and it is DERIVED rather than guessed**: the parser stamps it on
// every function it builds (`parseFunction`, plus `parseClosureExpression` for a lifted closure) and the
// two doors a synthesized function comes through — `IrFunction.createFromStd` and `ModuleInit`'s
// `IrFunction.create` — leave it empty. Nothing between there and here can lose it: the ONE boundary that
// COPIES a function is per-file artifact → merged module (`IrModule.merge` → `relocateFunction` →
// `shallowCopy`, which carries `sourceFilePath`/`nameLine`/`nameColumn` across), and the Maxon→Std transfer
// copies nothing at all — `lowerMaxonToStd` pushes the SAME `IrFunction` object, rewriting only its type
// fields. That is the SAME fact `TargetPrinter.isRuntimeFunction` reads, and it is read the
// same way for the same reason: a NAME test cannot do this job, because the one file that can reach this
// error is the one file whose declarations wear the compiler's own prefix (the A1r review measured a
// SIGSEGV on exactly that confusion).
//
// ⚠ **REFUSE, NEVER REDIRECT.** The A1r review established that steering the CALL to the surviving
// definition trades this refusal for a silent miscompile — `print("x")` would bind to the author's
// declaration instead of the runtime's `__print_string`, which knows the String record's layout. So the
// compile is refused at the duplicate, before isel, and no binary is produced either way.
function refuseDuplicateFunctionName(module StdModule, project Project, laterIndex FuncIndex) throws CompileError
	let later = module.getFunc(laterIndex)
	let earlier = earlierFunctionNamed(module, name: later.name, before: laterIndex)
	let name = String.from(later.name)

	if earlier.sourceFilePath.isEmpty() != later.sourceFilePath.isEmpty() 'oneOfEachProvenance'
		// The parsed side carries the author's position; the synthesized side's is blank by construction,
		// and an unpositioned diagnostic is barely better than the panic it replaces.
		let declared = later if earlier.sourceFilePath.isEmpty() else earlier
		reportPositioned(project, code: ErrorCode.declarationCollidesWithEmittedSymbol, message: "declaration of '{name}' collides with a symbol the compiler EMITS into this program, and a program cannot hold two functions of one name. The compiler's own definition is the one its emitted code is bound to — the entry stub's calls, the runtime's own call sites, and every call it lowers to that name — so the declaration is the side that must move: rename it", line: declared.nameLine, column: declared.nameColumn, filePath: declared.sourceFilePath)
		throw CompileError.compileError
	end 'oneOfEachProvenance'

	if later.sourceFilePath.isEmpty() 'bothSynthesized'
		panic("refuseDuplicateFunctionName: two SYNTHESIZED functions are named '{name}' — every runtime symbol is installed at exactly one site, so this is an installer that ran twice (or a compile driven twice over one memoized module), and the name index can only keep one of them")
	end 'bothSynthesized'

	// Both positions are printed rather than one cause asserted: two declarations that E3006 missed and ONE
	// declaration merged (or lowered) twice both land here, and only the two positions tell them apart. This
	// rung exists because a panic named two causes that were both false for the case in front of it.
	panic("refuseDuplicateFunctionName: two PARSED functions are named '{name}', at {earlier.sourceFilePath.toString()}:{earlier.nameLine} and {later.sourceFilePath.toString()}:{later.nameLine} — two DISTINCT positions mean `commitFuncSignatures`' whole-program E3006 did not see one of them and the pipeline's error gate let the module through; the SAME position twice means one declaration was merged or lowered into the module twice")
end 'refuseDuplicateFunctionName'

// The function `name` was already bound to before index `before`. It has to be recovered from the function
// ARRAY rather than the map: `upsert`'s last-wins has already replaced the incumbent's index with
// `before`'s, and on the diagnosable cause the incumbent is usually the very side that carries the author's
// position — installers append AFTER the user's source, so the parsed function is the earlier one.
//
// Linear, and that costs nothing: it runs at most once per compile, on a path whose next act is to refuse
// the compile or panic.
function earlierFunctionNamed(module StdModule, name ByteArray, before FuncIndex) returns IrFunction
	for i in 0 upto before 'eachEarlier'
		let candidate = module.getFunc(i)
		if candidate.name == name 'sameName'
			return candidate
		end 'sameName'
	end 'eachEarlier'

	panic("earlierFunctionNamed: the name index collapsed two entries at function {before} ('{String.from(name)}'), but no function before it carries that name — `upsert` overwrote an entry this scan cannot account for")
end 'earlierFunctionNamed'

// ⭐ **THE ROOT SET — the three reference kinds no Std op in this module spells.** Everything else is an
// ordinary call-graph edge and is found by the walk.
//
// 1. **`main`** — the program's entry. The entry stub that calls it (`prependEntryStub`,
//    `prependLinuxEntryStub`, arm64's hand-assembled chunk, wasm's synthesized `$run`) is built AFTER
//    this pass, at the Target tier, so its call is invisible here.
//
// 2. **The functions an ENTRY STUB or a hand-assembled Target-tier CHUNK calls by name.** Both are built
//    after this pass — the stubs in `augmentWithRuntime`/`buildWasmCoreModule`, the chunks as raw bytes
//    with `CallFixup`s resolved at concat — so their edges likewise cannot be walked. They were found by
//    enumerating every `targetName:`/`callDirect` in the stub and chunk builders:
//      * `__module_init` and `__maxon_global_cleanup` — the managed-global initialization and release the
//        entry stub brackets `main` with (P1.7 slice 2a, see `ModuleInit`). Nothing in the module calls
//        either, and they are what makes a managed global's `.data` slot non-null and its record freed —
//        so pruning one would turn a leak-gated spec into a null-pointer store or an exit-101 leak.
//      * `__mm_leak_check` — both entry stubs' leak gate (`appendLeakGate`, `Arm64Runtime:103`). Nothing
//        in the module CALLS it, so it survives only by being rooted here. ⭐ Its absence is LOUD on
//        every backend, and that is a property worth keeping deliberately: each gate emits its call from
//        `RuntimeUsage.usesHeap` — where installation is decided — never from "is the function still
//        there?", so a pruned root leaves a call with no callee. MEASURED by deleting this line:
//        x64 `resolveCallFixups: call to unknown function '__mm_leak_check'` (X64Backend:1794), arm64
//        `arm64ResolveCallFixups: bl to unknown function` (Arm64Backend:1420), wasm the `usesHeap` panic
//        in `StdToWasm.appendLeakGate`. ⚠ Restore that property if you touch a gate: no committed spec
//        makes the leak gate FIRE, so a gate that decided from the module instead would let this root's
//        removal turn every exit-101 test silently green.
//      * `__gt_enqueue` — the IOCP completion thread's cross-thread wake (`X64GtRuntime:772`).
//    They are rooted unconditionally: a name absent from this module is simply not found (a program
//    with no heap has no `__mm_leak_check` to keep), so the root costs nothing where the runtime it
//    belongs to was never installed.
//
// 3. **Every function named by an `.rdata` slot** (`GlobalDataTable.pendingRdataRelocs`) — a layout
//    descriptor's `destroyFunc@40`/`copyFunc@48` and every witness-table method slot. These are
//    reached through DATA, not code: `witnessCall` loads a `.rdata` table and jumps, and the element
//    drop walk `callIndirect`s a pointer baked into `.rdata` by `bakeFuncAbs64Relocs`. This is exactly
//    the precedent `noteDestructorUsage` already set — "referenced from data = live" — and because
//    this pass never prunes `.rdata` (see the header), rooting ALL of them is not conservatism, it is
//    the only correct answer: the slot survives, so its target must be there to fill it.
function seedRoots(globalData GlobalDataTable, byName FuncIndexByName, reached BoolArray, worklist FuncIndexArray)
	markLiveFunction(UserEntryName.toByteArray(), byName: byName, reached: reached, worklist: worklist)
	markLiveFunction(ModuleInitName, byName: byName, reached: reached, worklist: worklist)
	markLiveFunction(GlobalCleanupName, byName: byName, reached: reached, worklist: worklist)
	markLiveFunction(MmLeakCheckName, byName: byName, reached: reached, worklist: worklist)
	markLiveFunction(GtEnqueueName, byName: byName, reached: reached, worklist: worklist)

	for reloc in globalData.pendingRdataRelocs 'eachRdataSlot'
		markLiveFunction(reloc.funcName, byName: byName, reached: reached, worklist: worklist)
	end 'eachRdataSlot'
end 'seedRoots'

// Mark `name` reachable and queue its body for the walk. A name this module does not define is not an
// error and not a miss: it is a hand-assembled chunk (`__gt_context_switch`, `__gt_trampoline`,
// `__gt_morestack`, `__io_completion_loop`) or a root whose runtime this program never installed. There
// is nothing to keep and nothing to drop, so it is simply not queued.
function markLiveFunction(name ByteArray, byName FuncIndexByName, reached BoolArray, worklist FuncIndexArray)
	let idx = try byName.get(name) otherwise 'notAModuleFunction'
		return
	end 'notAModuleFunction'

	let alreadyReached = try reached.get(idx) otherwise panic("eliminateDeadFunctions: reached.get OOB at function index {idx}")
	if alreadyReached 'already'
		return
	end 'already'

	try reached.set(idx, value: true) otherwise panic("eliminateDeadFunctions: reached.set OOB at function index {idx}")
	worklist.push(idx)
end 'markLiveFunction'

// Every static callee edge out of one live function. Walks LIVE ops (`IrModule.liveOpIndices`), never
// the flat `module.ops` array: that array is append-only and holds the ops every prior block-rebuilding
// pass replaced, so scanning it would resurrect callees from IR that no longer exists.
function markCalleesOf(module StdModule, func IrFunction, byName FuncIndexByName, reached BoolArray, worklist FuncIndexArray)
	for opIdx in module.liveOpIndices(func) 'eachOp'
		let op = try module.ops.get(opIdx) otherwise panic("eliminateDeadFunctions: live op index {opIdx} not in module.ops")
		markCalleeOf(op, byName: byName, reached: reached, worklist: worklist)
	end 'eachOp'
end 'markCalleesOf'

// The static reachability edge one `StdOp` names, if any. THREE variants carry a function NAME —
// `call`, `tryCall` and `funcAddr` — and every one of them is an edge:
//
//   * `call` / `tryCall` — the direct call graph.
//   * `funcAddr` — a function's ADDRESS, and it is a STATIC edge even though the call that consumes it
//     is indirect. It is the whole of shv2's first-class-function support: `let f = triple` lowers to
//     `funcAddr(__fnref_triple)`, whose thunk body then `call`s `triple`; an async spawn takes the
//     spawned function's bare address; an element destructor is stamped into a descriptor slot the same
//     way. ⚠ v1 shipped this arm MISSING (its `funcAddrCalleeEdge` header records the fix): it assumed
//     a `funcAddr` target was already rooted by the `funcRef` that produced its value, which is false
//     for a directly-emitted one, and pruned the destructor.
//
// `callIndirect` and `witnessCall` deliberately name NO callee: they consume a code pointer that some
// `funcAddr` (marked above) or some `.rdata` slot (rooted in `seedRoots`) already produced, so crediting
// them would be crediting the same edge twice with no name to credit it to.
//
// The arms RANGE over the whole union in declaration order, ending at the current last variant, so a
// variant appended past it FAILS TO COMPILE here rather than being silently swallowed — the band-append
// invariant working in the reviewer's favour (StdDialect's header). A new variant that carries a callee
// name must be added as its own arm.
function markCalleeOf(op StdOp, byName FuncIndexByName, reached BoolArray, worklist FuncIndexArray)
	match op 'op'
		// arith + control + the call band up to `call`: pure computation, branches, `ret`, `param`.
		const to param then break 'op'
		call(_, callee, _) then markLiveFunction(callee, byName: byName, reached: reached, worklist: worklist)
		// `retVoid` … `rdataAddr`: returns, `.data`/`.rdata` ADDRESSES (labels, not functions), loads,
		// stores, and the two OS primitives that name no Maxon function.
		retVoid to rdataAddr then break 'op'
		tryCall(_, _, callee, _) then markLiveFunction(callee, byName: byName, reached: reached, worklist: worklist)
		// `errorReturn` reads two local virtuals; `osExit` is an OS primitive.
		errorReturn to osExit then break 'op'
		funcAddr(_, funcName) then markLiveFunction(funcName, byName: byName, reached: reached, worklist: worklist)
		// `callIndirect`/`witnessCall` (see above) plus every appended `os*` primitive and `switch`,
		// none of which names a Maxon function.
		callIndirect to osWriteFile then break 'op'
		// The wall-clock read names no Maxon function either — its callee is a Win32 import slot.
		osReadWallClock then break 'op'
		// The THROWING witness call names no Maxon function, exactly as `witnessCall` does not: both read a
		// code pointer out of a `.rdata` witness table, and the DATA edge that keeps a witness impl live is
		// counted by the witness-table walk, not here.
		witnessTryCall then break 'op'
		// Every file op calls a Win32 IMPORT, not a Maxon function, so none keeps anything live here
		// (R4.1 + R4.2).
		osFileOpenRead to osFileAttributes then break 'op'
	end 'op'
end 'markCalleeOf'

// Rebuild `module.functions` keeping only what the walk reached.
//
// ⚠ **A REBUILD IS A RENUMBERING, and `module.functions` is not the only thing keyed by that numbering.**
// `Project.valueOrigins` (and the parameter spans inside it) file every row under a function's POSITION
// in this array, so dropping a function silently re-points every later row at its neighbour. The remap
// below is part of the sweep, not a courtesy after it — see `ValueOriginTable.remapFunctionIndices`.
function sweepUnreachedFunctions(module StdModule, reached BoolArray, project Project)
	let total = module.funcCount()
	var kept = IrFunctionArray.create()
	// Old index → new index, filled for EVERY old index so the remap can index it directly; the entries
	// for dropped functions are never read (the remap consults `reached` first).
	var newIndexOfOld = FuncIndexArray.create()
	// Did dropping renumber any function that SURVIVES? Only a survivor whose index moved has an origin
	// row to relocate; a drop with no survivor after it moves nothing (see the trailing-drop skip below).
	var keptNumberingShifted = false
	for i in 0 upto total 'eachFunc'
		let func = module.getFunc(i)
		let isLive = try reached.get(i) otherwise panic("eliminateDeadFunctions: reached.get OOB at function index {i}")
		let newIndex = kept.count()
		newIndexOfOld.push(newIndex)
		if isLive 'live'
			if newIndex != i 'moved'
				keptNumberingShifted = true
			end 'moved'
			kept.push(func)
		end 'live' else 'dead'
			// `String.from` runs INSIDE the lazy message, never beside it: this arm is taken once per
			// function the pass drops, so materializing the name eagerly would spend an allocation per
			// SUCCESS — the cost rising with exactly how well the prune works — to render a line that is
			// off on every build but a `--log=ir:trace` one.
			logTrace(LogCategory.ir, message: function() gives "dfe: dropping unreachable function '{String.from(func.name)}'")
		end 'dead'
	end 'eachFunc'

	let dropped = total - kept.count()
	logDebug(LogCategory.ir, message: function() gives "dfe: dropped {dropped} unreachable function(s) of {total}")
	module.functions = kept

	// Nothing dropped ⇒ `newIndexOfOld` is the identity and no row loses its function, so the remap could
	// only rebuild the origin columns into an exact copy of themselves. Skipped rather than absorbed into
	// `remapFunctionIndices`, because the saving is the whole REBUILD (the columns are sized by the
	// program's value count, so the copy is linear in program size): measured +487,704 … +20,528,128 bytes
	// across the scale ladder, all of it on a corpus that drops nothing.
	if dropped == 0 'numberingUnchanged'
		return
	end 'numberingUnchanged'

	// ⭐ TRAILING DROPS ARE FREE TOO. When every dropped function sits AFTER every survivor — no
	// survivor's index moved — the remap would keep each survivor's rows at their existing index and
	// merely discard the trailing dropped rows. But those rows are `lookup`ed by (funcIndex, valueId)
	// and a dropped function's index is now beyond `module.functions`, so no survivor's lookup can ever
	// match one: leaving them costs a few extra iterations on the cold E5001 path and nothing else,
	// where rebuilding the program-sized columns costs the same linear bytes the `dropped == 0` case
	// avoids. Compiling stdlib source into every program makes this the common case: a stdlib module the
	// program does not use (e.g. Clock in a program that reads no clock) merges after the user source and
	// drops as a block, so a program that installs no emitted runtime (the runtime functions append AFTER
	// it) drops ONLY trailing functions — without this skip every such compile would pay the full rebuild
	// that loading stdlib newly forced on it. A program that DOES install runtime drops the module from
	// the MIDDLE
	// (the runtime sits after it), a survivor's index moves, and the remap correctly runs.
	if not keptNumberingShifted 'trailingDropsOnly'
		return
	end 'trailingDropsOnly'

	project.valueOrigins.remapFunctionIndices(reached, newIndexOfOld: newIndexOfOld)
end 'sweepUnreachedFunctions'
